leap71/PicoGKRuntime @ 0f26321c18ed (26.2): store lattice beams by value.

Proposal only. picovoxel, an unofficial community TypeScript/WebAssembly binding
of PicoGK (not affiliated with or endorsed by LEAP 71), applies the same change
to its vendored runtime as patches/PicoGKRuntime/0004-lattice-beam-value-
storage.patch. That file does not apply to a pristine tree, because its
trailing context includes the PruneFill() call added by picovoxel's 0002 patch.
This file is the same change rebased onto pristine 0f26321c18ed: the same 21
changed lines, with only hunk offsets and trailing context differing.

The change. Lattice stores std::vector<LatticeBeam::Ptr>: one make_shared
(control block plus the object) per beam and a pointer chase per beam in
RenderLattice. LatticeBeam has no virtual members and no shared owner anywhere
in the runtime, so std::vector<LatticeBeam> with emplace_back does the same job.

Output values: unchanged. Same beams, same order; picovoxel's pinned grids and
test suite did not move.

Measured (picovoxel, WebAssembly build, Apple M2 Pro): under 2 ns per beam
recovered on either allocator (1.05x and 1.09x, paired, 400k beams). The case
for the change is the contiguous beam array (a single buffer a GPU upload or a
bulk reader can use directly) and simpler ownership, more than speed.

Ordering with picogkruntime-tubecomplex-lattice.patch: either can land first;
that patch reads beams through overloads for both storage forms. Ordering with
picovoxel's 0002 post-fill prune: the two touch adjacent lines, so whichever
lands second needs a trivial rebase.

Applies with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` to a pristine PicoGKRuntime 0f26321c tree.
Not applied by picovoxel's build; see upstream/README.md.

